window.docContents[193].resolve({"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Logical-Connectives-as-Inductive-Types--Existential-Quantification":{"contents":"\n\nTo give evidence for an existential quantifier, we package a\nwitness x together with a proof that x satisfies the property\nP:namespace ExistsPlayground\ninductive Exists {α : Type} (P : α → Prop) : Prop where\n  | intro : ∀ (w : α), P w → Exists P\nend ExistsPlayground\nThis probably needs a little unpacking.  The core definition is\nfor a type former Exists that can be used to build propositions of\nthe form Exists P, where P itself is a function from witness\nvalues in the type α to propositions.  The Exists.intro\nconstructor then offers a way of constructing evidence for Exists p,\ngiven a witness w and a proof of P w.The notation in the standard library is a slight extension of\nthe above, enabling syntactic forms such as ∃ x y, P x y.\nThe standard library's Exists also takes P implicitly.The more familiar form ∃ x, Even x desugars to an expression\ninvolving Exists:#check (Exists (fun n => Even n) : Prop)\n∃ n, Even n : Prop\nHere's how to define an explicit proof object involving Exists:theorem some_nat_is_even : ∃ n, Even n :=\n  Exists.intro 4\n    (Even.succ_succ 2 (Even.succ_succ 0 Even.zero))\n\n\n\n\nWhich of the following propositions is proved by\nproviding an explicit witness w using exists w?(A) ∀ (x : Nat), (∃ n, x = n + 1) → x ≠ 0(B) ∀ (x : Nat), x ≠ 0 → (∃ n, x = n + 1)(C) ∀ (x : Nat), x = 0 → ¬(∃ n, x = n + 1)(D) ∀ (x : Nat), x = 4 → x ≠ 0(E) none of the aboveexample : ∀ (x : Nat), x ≠ 0 → (∃ n, x = n + 1) := by\n  intro x h\n  cases x with\n  | zero =>\n    exfalso\n    apply h\n    rfl\n  | succ x' => exists x'\n\n\nConstruct a proof object for the following proposition.theorem ex_even_Sn : Exists (fun n => Even (n + 1)) :=\n  solution!(Exists.intro 1 (Even.succ_succ 0 Even.zero))\n\n\n\n\nTo destruct existentials in a proof term we simply use match:\n\ntheorem dist_exists_or_term (α : Type) (P Q : α → Prop) :\n    (∃ x, P x ∨ Q x) → (∃ x, P x) ∨ (∃ x, Q x) :=\n  fun h => match h with\n           | Exists.intro x hx =>\n               match hx with\n               | Or.inl hpx => Or.inl (Exists.intro x hpx)\n               | Or.inr hqx => Or.inr (Exists.intro x hqx)\n\n\nConstruct a proof object for the following proposition:theorem ex_match : ∀ (A : Type) (P Q : A → Prop),\n    (∀ (x : A), P x → Q x) →\n    (∃ x, P x) → (∃ x, Q x) :=\n  solution!(fun _ _ _ hpq hp =>\n    match hp with\n    | Exists.intro x hpx => Exists.intro x (hpq x hpx))\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009ProofObjects: The Curry-Howard Correspondence\u0009Logical Connectives as Inductive Types","header":"10.6.3. Existential Quantification","id":"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Logical-Connectives-as-Inductive-Types--Existential-Quantification"}});